From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:51:07 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] Windows 10 Mobile: H.264 on the GPU's decoder, one video at a
 time

Chromium's D3D11VideoDecoder, on ANGLE's device, in a process that is the
whole browser and on Adreno drivers gecko-w10m has met:

- One video at a time has the GPU's decoder; the others are refused in
  Initialize() and go on to the software decoder. One decoder of the
  driver's exists at a time: none is made from Initialize(), the old one is
  released and flushed before the next is made, and another video's waits
  until the last has been gone a second. (A second DXVA session beside the
  first, or right after it, ran the Adreno 530 driver's stack out under
  gecko-w10m.)
- The driver is asked what it decodes on ANGLE's own device, and no H.264
  is offered unless it lists a configuration with short slices, the only
  form this decoder sends. What it answers is in the log.
- With frames copied out by the video processor (the embedder's default),
  the decoder's textures are made for decoding only.
- The embedder is told when the driver is first asked and when frames have
  come out for a while (ui/gl/w10m_panel.h), and counts the runs that end
  in between.
- Where upstream loses a GPU process, this build would lose the browser:
  four CHECKs on the path are failures instead, a driver that stays busy
  fails the decode after two seconds, sizes beyond 3840x2160 are not
  probed.
- A decoder that fails after its first frame, or whose GL has been given
  up, has the page's player start again (it then gets the software
  decoder) instead of showing an error: DecoderStream keeps the failure's
  code for the next Read().

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
